---
import SupportingGuide from "@components/SupportingGuide.astro";
const faq = "https://www.weedkillerclass.com/Home/FAQs";
---
<SupportingGuide path="/lawsuits/roundup/deadline/" introduction="A Roundup deadline can mean the time to file an individual lawsuit, a settlement participation deadline, or a date in an existing court case. Identify the process before relying on a calendar date.">
  <section id="three-clocks">
    <h2>Separate the three kinds of dates</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <thead><tr class="border-b border-line"><th class="p-3">Date type</th><th class="p-3">What to check</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-line"><th class="p-3">Individual lawsuit</th><td class="p-3">Applicable state law, the relevant starting date, and any exception or tolling agreement.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-line"><th class="p-3">Settlement participation</th><td class="p-3">The administrator's current notice, class definition, registration instructions, and claim package rules.</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-line"><th class="p-3">Existing court case</th><td class="p-3">The order governing that case and the people or filings to which it applies.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4">The settlement's official notice distinguishes state time limits from its own application process. A hearing date alone does not supply the information needed to calculate either deadline. <a href={faq}>Source: official settlement FAQ, questions 10, 18 and 26</a>.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="settlement-dates">
    <h2>How to read the settlement schedule</h2>
    <p>The official FAQ ties registration for the already-diagnosed subclass to final court approval and ties its claim package to the settlement becoming final. These are different triggers. It says exact calendar dates will be posted after approval. Use the <a href="https://www.weedkillerclass.com/">official settlement website</a> and governing orders for the operative dates; do not calculate them from an old hearing announcement. <a href={faq}>Source: official settlement FAQ, questions 10 and 26</a>.</p>
    <p>Save the notice version and the date you reviewed it. For any deadline, write down four things: who it applies to, the required action, the event that starts the period, and the official source. If those items are unclear, ask the administrator about its procedure and a lawyer about the effect on your rights.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="state-law">
    <h2>Why the state filing deadline needs its own review</h2>
    <p>California's court self-help guide illustrates the issue: it lists a general personal-injury period, explains delayed discovery, and describes circumstances that may pause a limitation period. It also cautions that applying these rules can be difficult. This is a California example, not a national Roundup deadline. <a href="https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/court-basics/statute-limitations">Source: California Courts, deadlines to sue</a>.</p>
    <p>For a review, assemble the diagnosis date, locations and years of exposure, residence history, dates of prior filings, and any signed tolling or release paperwork. Flag uncertain dates rather than filling in an assumed date. The <a href="/lawsuits/roundup/proof/">Roundup proof guide</a> helps organize those records.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="deadline-review">
    <h2>What to confirm for an existing claim</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc">
      <li>Which court and case number apply to the claim?</li>
      <li>Which notice or order created the deadline, and has it been amended?</li>
      <li>Does a lawyer have a record of a filing, submission, or signed agreement?</li>
      <li>Is the date a hearing, a document deadline, or a deadline to begin a lawsuit?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The <a href="/mdl/2741/">MDL 2741 guide</a> identifies the federal proceeding. The <a href="/lawsuits/roundup/">national Roundup guide</a> tracks broader developments, including the separately administered settlement. Neither page can confirm that an individual claim remains timely.</p>
  </section>
</SupportingGuide>
